WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:11.780
What if I told you that as many as 8.8 million unique viewers were watching the same Fortnite competition every Friday.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.620
And that it wasn't even run by Fortnite developer Epic Games, but by an unaffiliated YouTuber.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.460
"Why hello there my name is
Keemstar."

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.320
And just recently, Epic pledged to sink $100,000,000 into the upcoming season.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.560
Birthing the Fornite World Cup sometime in the future and the 8-week Fornite Summer Skirmish.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.140
Whether it’s esports or not this shit is popping off.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:48.020
Fortnite Friday answered one of the big questions that everyone had when Fornite exploded into mainstream relevance just a few months ago.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.140
How are they going to make this interesting to watch?

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:57.140
Unlike other attempts at Battle Royale esports, Fortnite Fridays has placed the emphasis on two things — streamers and kills.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.340
"Excuse me?"

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.020
The whole thing feels just as much like a reality show as it does a major esports event.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:14.500
There's as much focus on the memes, the highlight plays and the spectacle that Fortnite has become on Twitch as there is on who wins and who loses.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:30.100
(Casting)

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.500
Which, is probably where all the “Fornite’s not an esport” cries are coming from.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.580
But, the answer to that question doesn’t really matter.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.580
The bottom line is that people are competing, and millions of eyes are on them.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:50.600
Problem number one with Battle Royale esports is the logistical nightmare with getting 100 people in a room, physically or virtually to battle to the death.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:56.480
The second big problem, is how you make that interesting for viewers without boring them to death or giving them a seizure.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:05.980
(Casting)

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.420
What popular Youtuber Keemstar and UMG have done with Fortnite Fridays is tune the focus of their broadcast away

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:16.180
from the highest tiers of competitive integrity and toward action and entertainment.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.640
Here’s how it works. During Fornite Fridays, two teams of two join a four person squads and battle it out.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.980
The team with the most eliminations moves on in the bracket.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.760
And every team MUST stream the matches to ensure competitive integrity, and well, viewership numbers.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.760
Gone are the problems with turtling-up in one-by-one structures and playing riskless Fortnite. This format is about lethality.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:39.840
"Stay on me."

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.680
"I headshotted him with a no-scope."

00:02:44.220 --> 00:03:02.380
(Casting)

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.960
But it’s not just Keemstar trying to make the competitive side of Fortnite work.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.920
Fornite’s esports origin story starts in the same place that almost everything important about Fortnite starts from.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.340
Ninja.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.540
Ninja hosted his own event, Ninja Vegas at the Esports Arena in Las Vegas.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:32.320
The event broke Ninja’s own record for concurrent viewers on an individual’s Twitch stream with 667,000 concurrent viewers.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:38.340
At the time, it wasn’t anything new in terms of format. Ninja mostly just applied the PUBG esports format to Fortnite.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.340
But just like Keemstar does now, he added an emphasis on personality.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:46.820
The event was about Ninja, players even got a financial incentive if they managed to kill him.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:53.920
But, a lot of the on-screen broadcast time was spent on other top streamers too, like Myth. DrLupo was even one of the casters.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.680
Fortnite has shown us that these on-screen personalities are what keep viewers watching.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.540
After Ninja Vegas came the E3 Fornite Invitational Pro-Am.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.940
Which leveraged the fact that even celebrities were caught up in the massive wave of

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.840
Fortnite culture. And teamed them up
with top streamers to compete.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:18.900
(Casting)

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.180
The event was our first opportunity to see Fortnite from
a third-person perspective, getting to

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.100
see build battles from afar without
having to deal with that spinning

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.040
first-person blueprint rollercoaster
ride we're all used to.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.100
By and large the event was successful widely viewed an
even relatively action-packed.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:38.460
And, in storybook fashion, Ninja and DJ Marshmellow came out the victors.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.760
"And here you have it guys the 2018 Pro-Am Fortnite tournament winners."

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.680
Now, Fortnite Friday has been a shocking success,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:52.400
Lil Yachty, Paul George and Marshmellow played at the Invitational and obviously Ninja Vegas was incredibly successful.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.500
So, is Fortnite the future of esports?

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.040
Well, it hasn’t all been perfect.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.080
Let’s get back to that  $100,000,000 investment Epic is making.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:06.820
It’s an exciting number, sure. But the implementation of the Fornite esports framework has been awkward…

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:14.120
"Dude I can't even move bro. I cannot even move bro. I cannot even move bro."

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.360
"One more time. Oh my god I can't even move!"

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.040
The Fornite Summer Skirmish is an 8-week-long summer tournament series that's happening every Friday and Saturday this summer.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.280
$8,000,000 in prize money is guaranteed over the course of the event.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:36.220
It sounds great right? Well even the first few weeks of the event have been mired in mixed reactions and severe technical problems.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.800
"I can't run out of the storm because it's lagging. I'm still stuck in the zone. Are you kidding?"

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:47.440
"Are you kidding? I'm in the- what was that? Are you kidd- I couldn't get in the storm."

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:49.360
"Look how many people just died to the zone."

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.620
Epic hit the right tone by allowing players to stream the tournament,

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.540
but their viewer-friendly esports client wasn’t exactly ready for the start of the season.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:03.340
There was lag, UI issues and going back to that focus on survival made the format just boring.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:09.180
The Summer Skirmish does offer a small incentive for payers who are able to get the most kills,

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.900
But, if we're being honest it really only matters if you can
get Victory Royales.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.680
It’s simply about staying alive, and sometimes that’s like watching paint dry.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.380
but Fortnite Friday never had that
problem.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:42.020
I think at this point it’s pretty hard to argue that Fortnite’s not well on its way to establishing itself as an esport...

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.800
But it is easy to argue that there’s a long way to go.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:50.680
Pro players are literally trying to queue up into a lobby all at the same time in hopes of getting an effective scrim.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:07:00.100
"5, 4, 3, 2, 1, go. Now, if i missed that one I call I just quit. Period."

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.500
And the game itself does absolutely nothing to help competitive players.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.100
There's no ranked mode, no custom lobbies, nothing. What we're seeing with Fortnite esports

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.800
is honestly not that far from the early days of of PUBG esports. where a

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.300
hundred players would load into a match at the same time leading to overheated

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:20.060
machines and failed events. The viewer experience was challenging to say the least.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:27.560
(Casting)

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.940
At the centre of it all seems to be streaming.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:34.560
Fortnite has proven that you don't need everybody all in the same place to compete. And that, when it comes

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.560
to viewership, personality and accessibility are often just as important as skill.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.120
Fortnite has also proven that as much as we want to watch our favourite players win,

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.260
we also want to be able to interact with them,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.020
we want to watch them style on people,

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.500
"Oh my! Turner!

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.600
We want to watch them dance and trash talk.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.620
"What's that sound? Are you dancing right now?"

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.700
We want to get to the heart of what gaming is all about.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.480
So is Fortnite an esport? Does it even matter?

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:02.480
"Dad!"

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.140
"Eighty six f***ing thousand dollars?"

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:07.060
"Yeah."

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.240
"Are you crying?"

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:09.760
"Little, yeah."

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.840
"Ha hey hey, I would too brother!"

